 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Architecture",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "A zero-drift difference amplifier, single supply, with a fixed gain of 20 V/V. Input offset drift is under 400 nV/°C without giving up bandwidth (250 kHz typical). Common-mode rejection holds with PWM common-mode inputs that slew fast, for example 1 V/ns."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Reference inputs",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "VREF1 and VREF2 each connect through a 100 kΩ resistor to the non-inverting input of the main amplifier, so the output's zero-current level can be set to any level in the output range. The two pins are interchangeable. Driven together they pass a reference to the output at 1 V/V; split between a voltage and ground they pass half of it (0.5 V/V)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Unidirectional operation",
   "page": 12,
   "text": "For current in one direction only, park the output at a rail with zero input: both reference pins to ground puts it at the low rail, and a positive input (+IN above −IN) drives it up; both to VS puts it at the high rail, and a negative input drives it down. The VS-referenced mode suits diagnostics that must detect the amplifier and wiring before the load is powered."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Bidirectional operation",
   "page": 12,
   "text": "For current in both directions, set the zero-current output somewhere inside the output range, usually mid-scale for equal range each way, or off-centre when the currents are not symmetric. Ways to do it: drive both reference pins from an external reference; tie one to VS and one to GND for a supply-ratiometric VS/2; or tie one to GND and one to an external reference to get half that reference with about 0.5 % accuracy."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Motor control",
   "page": 14,
   "text": "In three-phase drives the shunt goes in a motor phase; the 250 kHz bandwidth tracks the current continuously, the low drift keeps phase-to-phase error small over temperature, and the reading flags shorts to ground or battery. PWM common mode from −2 V to +70 V is rejected with a 5 V supply. In an H-bridge the shunt sits between the bridge legs in series with the motor, which avoids relying on a ground reference that is not stable in such a system; the output then runs externally referenced and bidirectional."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Solenoid control",
   "page": 15,
   "text": "With the shunt placed above the solenoid, the recirculating current through the clamp diode still passes the shunt when the switch is off, so the full current is measured and shorts to ground can be diagnosed. With a low-side switch the common mode swings between near ground and one diode above the battery; with a high-side switch it swings between the battery voltage and one diode below ground. A high-side switch also keeps the battery off the load most of the time, cutting corrosion and the chance of unwanted actuation. With the shunt referenced to the battery (high rail sensing), the output is a linear ground-referenced voltage; an AD8214 on the same shunt can add an overcurrent flag within 100 ns."
  },
  {
   "topic": "FMEA pinout",
   "page": 16,
   "text": "The 10-lead MSOP places NC pins between −IN and GND and between +IN and VREF1, so a solder bridge from an input, which may sit anywhere from −2 V to +70 V, lands on an unused pin rather than causing a fault the part cannot recover from. The pinout is aimed at automotive single-fault tolerance against common board defects."
  }
 ],
